[38;2;255;255;255m●[39m No. According to HiveMind, the SQLite decision has been
  replaced, though the replacement isn't finished yet.

  [1mThe original decision[0m ([38;2;177;185;249mdecision-44c8ffcb[39m, your call): use
  SQLite as a single file next to the app. The JSON file was
  losing writes when two requests came in at once, and you
  rejected Postgres as overkill for a one-person app because
  it's a server to run and back up. You also rejected keeping
  the JSON file.

  [1mWhy[0m [1mit[0m [1mdidn't[0m [1mhold:[0m it's marked [1msuperseded[0m by
  [38;2;177;185;249mdecision-d15fa52a[39m, "Use Postgres on the home server for pantry
  storage." The new requirement is using pantry from both a
  phone and a laptop, and one SQLite file can't be shared
  between devices. That decision accepts the cost of running and
  backing up a server, which was the reason you rejected
  Postgres in the first place. So the concurrency reasoning was
  sound; the "one device, one user" assumption is what changed.

  [1mThe[0m [1mPostgres[0m [1mdecision[0m [1misn't[0m [1msettled[0m [1myet:[0m
  - Its status is [1mproposed[0m, not accepted. The SQLite decision
    already shows as superseded, but nothing has confirmed its
    replacement.
  - The code still uses SQLite. The latest commit is [38;2;177;185;249mc6aa80a[39m
    [38;2;177;185;249mstore[39m [38;2;177;185;249mitems[39m [38;2;177;185;249min[39m [38;2;177;185;249mSQLite[39m [38;2;177;185;249m(pantry.db)[39m, and nothing after it
    moves to Postgres.
  - Neither decision has any evidence attached, so neither is
    backed by an observed failure or measurement.

  If you're committing to Postgres, the next steps are to accept
  that decision and then do the migration. If you're not sure
  yet, the phone-plus-laptop need might be met without running a
  database server, for example by hosting the Flask app itself
  on the home server and keeping SQLite behind it.

